<html xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word"><head><meta charset=utf-8><title>Paul Banyard - Full Pack Part 8</title></head><body><h1>Paul Banyard - Full Pack Part 8</h1><p>Paul Banyard — Full Notes &amp; Transcripts (Part 8)</p><p>Compiled from Plaud. Includes meeting notes and full transcripts. Being looked at and reviewed.</p><p>07-01 Case Management</p><p>Plaud file id: dab8fbd8fe0d9cb4221578dd6b287d69</p><p>Notes</p><p>### Summary</p><p># Analysis of Mortgage Case Management and Workflow Optimisation</p><p>This document outlines a series of interconnected workplace conversations focused on resolving complex mortgage application issues, managing internal workflows, and coordinating team tasks. Key discussions revolve around a specific client&#x27;s HSBC application, which was complicated by misunderstandings about the applicants&#x27; details and property usage, alongside a broader review of individual task management, workflow efficiency, and inter-team communication protocols.</p><p>---</p><p>## Discrepancy Resolution on a Joint Mortgage Application</p><p>The initial conflict arises over missing documentation and case details on the platform. Megan Gordon explains that the case documentation was not appearing on her desk because the transfer process on the platform was incomplete. Bob Duncan argues that the client&#x27;s wife must be named on the application as an occupier because she is over the age of seventeen. During the exchange, Megan acknowledges her error regarding the omission of the daughter, stating, &quot;I wasn&#x27;t aware of the daughters... so I&#x27;ll take that on the chin as that was my fault.&quot; Bob accuses Megan of not doing her job, but Megan maintains the issue stems from the platform&#x27;s case transfer process. Ultimately, the team identifies the need to rectify the application by ensuring the wife is listed as an occupier over 17 and that the daughter is added to the system.</p><p>---</p><p>## General Team Coordination and Office Banter</p><p>Alongside case work, the team engages in general coordination and office banter. Megan mentions that an expected baby is seven and a half pounds at thirty-six weeks gestation. A discussion about monetary contributions for a colleague collection reveals that Rowan gave £21, Holly gave £22, and Paul contributed an additional £5. Bob notes that managing people can be difficult and decides to leave the Saturday worker&#x27;s diary open for the time being, playfully expressing an intention to leave it open &quot;to annoy her that little bit further.&quot; The team also coordinates obtaining Sean&#x27;s address to send him gift vouchers.</p><p>---</p><p>## Strategic Review of a Complex HSBC Application with Paul</p><p>Bob and Paul Banyard engage in a detailed strategic review of a problematic HSBC mortgage case. Bob explains that the client is resident in America, which led the lender to erroneously believe the application was for a buy-to-let purchase. Furthermore, the application was initially submitted as joint and then resubmitted as sole; the lender misinterpreted this as a deliberate change to disguise a buy-to-let. The application was automatically declined because the clients already own two properties, though Paul notes that HSBC policy includes an exception to this limit for high-net-worth individuals. Paul theorises that the underwriter is likely inexperienced, misinterpreting details while thinking &quot;they&#x27;re being fucking smart.&quot; He criticises the lender for generating a narrative of mortgage fraud based entirely on their own error, arguing that lenders should utilise experienced underwriters capable of &#x27;judgmental lending&#x27; rather than just reading policy verbatim. He suggests escalating the issue, remarking, &quot;In other words, You&#x27;re a shower of shit and I&#x27;m telling your senior management that as well. Basically, expect a phone call in the next day.&quot;</p><p>---</p><p>## Paul&#x27;s Workflow and Task Management Review</p><p>Bob conducts a review of Paul&#x27;s workflow and task management. Paul explains that his backlog initially consisted of twenty-seven tasks appearing as a large block. He points out that while he could have cleared his task backlog to zero by simply emailing and diarising items to make the queue look cleaner, he prefers to actively work through the cases, which is a slower but more thorough process. Bob introduces a workflow update that requires specific steps for document requests and lead nurturing, including a &#x27;manage lead&#x27; option. Bob notes that the platform does not auto-refresh tasks, requiring manual management, and emphasises that effective lead nurturing involves structured &#x27;nurture reasons&#x27; and consistent follow-up communications. Paul suggests the platform would be more efficient if it auto-refreshed and if the email interface allowed for viewing more content without scrolling. They agree on a workflow process for managing &#x27;No Contact&#x27; leads, moving them to &#x27;nurture&#x27; and adding specific internal notes.</p><p>---</p><p>## Analysis of Specific Client Cases and Lending Scenarios</p><p>The team discusses several specific client scenarios. Paul mentions a 69-year-old client seeking a mortgage with a term extending to age 80. He notes that LiveMore accepts office-based/non-manual roles up to age 80 (capping manual jobs at 75), whereas many mainstream high street lenders will not use employed income for terms beyond age 75, restricting borrowing to pension income. Bob advises that they shouldn&#x27;t waste time chasing clients who claim another broker has already secured a lender for complex cases. Another case involves a first-time buyer (Gary) who intended to buy a residential property, renovate it, and remortgage it as a buy-to-let within 12 months. Paul identifies this as a compliance and mortgage fraud risk, recommending the use of legitimate refurbishment mortgage products instead. Finally, Paul discusses a product transfer case where the lender (Halifax) required affordability documentation even though it&#x27;s often not possible to vary the mortgage term during a product transfer.</p><p>---</p><p>## Action Items</p><p>**@Bob Duncan**</p><p>- [ ] Draft email to Victoria - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Obtain Sean&#x27;s address to facilitate gift delivery - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Close Callum&#x27;s diary - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Acquire reference number for HSBC application - [TBD]</p><p>**@Megan Gordon**</p><p>- [ ] Update the application to include the daughter as an occupier and ensure all files are correctly linked and transferred on the platform - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Purchase and post gift vouchers to Sean upon receipt of address - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Arrange deductions for Rowan - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Move account/case information over - [TBD]</p><p>**@Paul Banyard**</p><p>- [ ] Ensure all tasks in the workflow are assigned to yourself when picking them up from the queue - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Add specific internal notes when moving a lead to the &#x27;nurture&#x27; category to ensure clear history and structure - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Inform the client (Gary) that the strategy to flip from residential to buy-to-let is not viable and that the advisor cannot risk his license to facilitate it - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Review case notes regarding the product transfer/term dispute to justify the advice given to the client - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Call client back at 2:00 PM - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Send update email to the team regarding mortgage updates and process - [TBD]</p><p>### ADVISER WEEKLY PERFORMANCE &amp; ACTION REVIEW</p><p># Weekly Performance Review – Paul Banyard</p><p>**Review Date:** 2026-07-01</p><p>---</p><p>## 1. FOLLOW-UP FROM PREVIOUS MEETING</p><p>**No formal actions from previous meeting were explicitly referenced in this session.**</p><p>However, ongoing task management issues were discussed (see Section 2).</p><p>---</p><p>## 2. LEADS PERFORMANCE REVIEW (WITH CRM DISCIPLINE)</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>- Paul has **27 platform tasks** currently in the system</p><p>- **8 tasks are currently late** (5 from 2026-07-01)</p><p>- Several tasks rolled over from 2026-06-30 that were not completed</p><p>- During the review, Paul actively cleared multiple tasks and consolidated duplicate entries</p><p>### What&#x27;s Working Well</p><p>- Paul is attempting to work through tasks &quot;the right way&quot; rather than just diarising them forward to make numbers look better</p><p>- Making phone calls before sending emails where possible</p><p>- Actively clearing out genuinely dead leads (2-3 binned where multiple contact attempts failed)</p><p>- Has made a &quot;big dent&quot; in the task list since previous review</p><p>- Notes are being added to client records</p><p>- Tasks are being reassigned appropriately to maintain ownership</p><p>- Paul is seeking guidance on complex cases before proceeding</p><p>### What&#x27;s Not Working Well</p><p>- **27 outstanding tasks** is significantly high and represents poor pipeline management</p><p>- **8 late tasks** shows SLA breaches and missed follow-up commitments</p><p>- Time in Edinburgh created backlog (excuse-led behaviour)</p><p>- Tasks from 2026-06-30 not completed – claiming they &quot;weren&#x27;t a massive priority&quot; shows poor judgement on urgency</p><p>- Slow progress rate due to methodology – could work faster</p><p>- **CRM workflow confusion:** Tasks being used incorrectly (e.g., Anna marked as &quot;documents requested&quot; when no documents were requested)</p><p>- System appears to be auto-generating duplicate tasks when moving cases forward (e.g., Gary Fraser case generated multiple tasks for the same action)</p><p>- Tasks not consistently being self-assigned when created</p><p>- Some tasks lack clear next action notes in brackets</p><p>### Specific Case Review</p><p>#### **Jimmy F – Documents Requested**</p><p>- Task shows &quot;documents in&quot; but Paul unclear on status</p><p>- New workflow now available: can move to &quot;awaiting documents&quot; stage and assign to Declan for follow-up</p><p>- **Risk:** Case drifting due to unclear ownership and next steps</p><p>#### **Anna (Female Client – Shetland/Glasgow)**</p><p>- **Client Background:** Ex-US military, now working for UN/EU, living in Shetland, renting in Glasgow, wants to buy in Glasgow</p><p>- **Lead Age:** Over 1 year old</p><p>- **Last Contact:** 2026-06-18 – advised not to start property search until after summer, but suggested starting groundwork now</p><p>- **Current Status:** Task created for &quot;documents requested&quot; but Paul confirms no documents were actually requested</p><p>- **Actual Situation:** Trying to arrange DIP before summer; previous DIP attempt declined due to credit scoring</p><p>- **Last Action:** Sent recap email, then follow-up asking to arrange catch-up and get DIP</p><p>- **CRM Issue:** Task incorrectly categorised as &quot;documents requested&quot; when it should be &quot;arrange DIP appointment&quot; or &quot;client chase&quot;</p><p>- **Next Step:** Paul plans to reach out again to arrange DIP</p><p>#### **Anna&#x27;s Client (separate case)**</p><p>- Outstanding matter requiring contact</p><p>- Task assigned for 2026-07-01 14:00</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- High task volume creates risk of leads going cold or converting elsewhere</p><p>- Late tasks damage client experience and conversion rates</p><p>- Pattern of justifying delays with excuses rather than proactive resolution</p><p>- Pipeline velocity is too slow – conversion opportunities being lost</p><p>- CRM workflow creating unnecessary administrative burden with duplicate tasks</p><p>- Risk of missing genuine follow-ups when multiple system-generated tasks exist for same client</p><p>- Lead over 1 year old (Anna) with limited progress</p><p>- Unclear case ownership and next steps (Jimmy F)</p><p>- Paul doesn&#x27;t fully understand new workflow options available to him</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not done |</p><p>| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |</p><p>| Clear all 8 late platform tasks | Platform task management | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-02 | Further SLA breaches; clients go cold; conversion loss |</p><p>| Reduce total platform tasks to under 15 | Platform task management | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-04 | Pipeline continues to drift; revenue risk increases |</p><p>| Contact Anna to arrange DIP call; if no response move to Scotland nurture | Anna (Shetland/Glasgow purchase) | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-03 | 1+ year lead becomes dead; opportunity lost |</p><p>| Clarify Jimmy F document status and progress case | Jimmy F | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-02 | Case stalls; unclear ownership creates gaps |</p><p>| Review all 27 tasks and ensure workflow stages accurately reflect case status | CRM discipline | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-03 | Incorrect data; poor handoffs; management can&#x27;t identify real risks |</p><p>| No platform tasks to be late beyond this date | Task discipline | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-04 | Culture of lateness becomes normalised; performance declines |</p><p>| Ensure all new tasks include next action in brackets and are self-assigned | All cases | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Tasks become unclear and drift |</p><p>| Contact client re: outstanding matter | Anna&#x27;s client | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-01 14:00 | Client disengagement |</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Declining with CRM Quality Issues** – Task volume has increased to 27 and lateness is occurring. Excuses are being made (Edinburgh trip, prioritisation judgement) rather than proactive management. Cases are not progressing efficiently and CRM workflow stages don&#x27;t accurately reflect reality, creating confusion and poor handoff capability. However, Paul is proactively managing tasks during review sessions, though system workflow issues are creating avoidable admin work.</p><p>---</p><p>## 4. APPLICATIONS IN PROGRESS</p><p>### **Cole – Age 69 Purchase Case**</p><p>**Client Name:** Cole</p><p>**Age:** 69 years old</p><p>**Lender:** Seeking lender (LiveMore identified as option)</p><p>**Current Stage:** Initial appointment completed last week; awaiting client decision</p><p>**Case Details:**</p><p>- Client wants to purchase a property</p><p>- Well-paid job, employed income</p><p>- Requires: ability to use employed income + mortgage term to age 80</p><p>**Issue:**</p><p>- Most mainstream high street lenders won&#x27;t use employed income beyond age 75 – only pension income</p><p>- Many lenders have &quot;within 10 years of retirement&quot; rule</p><p>- **LiveMore** will:</p><p>  - Use employed income for non-manual (office-based) jobs to age 80</p><p>  - Use employed income for manual jobs to age 75</p><p>  - No &quot;within 10 years of retirement&quot; restriction</p><p>  - **However:** Rates are 6-6.5% (significantly higher than high street)</p><p>**Client Behaviour:**</p><p>- Cole told Paul he&#x27;d &quot;spoken to another broker who says he&#x27;s got a lender that will do it&quot;</p><p>- Paul&#x27;s reaction: questioned why client is wasting his time if another broker has a solution</p><p>**Current Position:**</p><p>- Paul attempted phone contact, couldn&#x27;t reach Cole</p><p>- Sent email confirming LiveMore as an option but highlighting high rates</p><p>- Client hasn&#x27;t responded since mentioning other broker</p><p>**What&#x27;s Not Working Well:**</p><p>- Client engagement is weak</p><p>- Paul spending time on low-probability case</p><p>**Risks / Blockers:**</p><p>- Client likely shopping around or has engaged another broker</p><p>- Low probability of conversion</p><p>- Time investment unlikely to yield return</p><p>**Actions Required:**</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not done |</p><p>| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |</p><p>| Send final email to Cole; if no response by 2026-07-04, mark lost and archive | Cole (age 69 purchase) | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-02 | Time wasted on dead case |</p><p>| **Trend Insight:** **Declining/Dead Case** – Client has disengaged and indicated they&#x27;re working with another broker. Minimal time investment warranted. |  |  |  |  |</p><p>---</p><p>### **Gary Fraser – First Time Buyer with BTL Conversion Intent**</p><p>**Current Stage:** Pre-DIP consultation</p><p>**Issue Identified:**</p><p>Client is a First Time Buyer (son of previous client) who wants to:</p><p>1. Purchase residential property at 85-90% LTV</p><p>2. Refurbish the property</p><p>3. Remortgage to BTL within 12 months on a tracker rate</p><p>**Compliance Risk:**</p><p>- Client&#x27;s stated intention to convert to BTL within 12 months creates a **regulatory and lender fraud risk**</p><p>- Original mortgage would be residential occupancy basis; converting to BTL breaches mortgage terms</p><p>- Paul correctly identified this as a risk to his authorisation</p><p>- System auto-generated multiple duplicate tasks when Paul moved this case forward</p><p>**Advice Given:**</p><p>- Bob Duncan confirmed this approach is **not permissible**</p><p>- Recommended exploring **refurbishment mortgage products** instead (likely 70-75% LTV, higher arrangement fees circa £500+)</p><p>- Paul to research specialist refurbishment lending options</p><p>- If no viable product exists, Paul must decline to proceed rather than structure a non-compliant mortgage</p><p>**What&#x27;s Working Well:**</p><p>- Paul correctly identified compliance risk before proceeding</p><p>- Seeking guidance on complex case</p><p>**Actions Required:**</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not done |</p><p>| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |</p><p>| Research refurbishment mortgage options for First Time Buyer scenario | Gary Fraser | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-07 | Client receives inappropriate advice or unviable product recommendation |</p><p>| Contact Gary Fraser and explain: original plan is not compliant; alternative refurbishment products available at lower LTV and higher cost; if client insists on original plan, Paul cannot assist | Gary Fraser | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-07 | Regulatory breach, potential lender fraud, loss of authorisation |</p><p>| **Trend Insight:** **Strong Compliance Awareness** – Paul is methodical and compliance-conscious when uncertain. Strong ethical boundaries (willing to decline business that creates regulatory risk). |  |  |  |  |</p><p>---</p><p>## 5. REFERRAL PIPELINE &amp; BUSINESS DEVELOPMENT</p><p>### Dalton Wybar Referral Relationship</p><p>**Current Position:**</p><p>- Paul submitted a mortgage application on 2026-06-30 for a referral from Gavin (Dalton Wybar) originally sent in March 2026</p><p>- Follow-up email sent to Gavin, CC&#x27;d to John Wybar</p><p>- Target discussed: **one referral per person per month from Dalton Wybar team**</p><p>- Paul attended Dalton Wybar office on 2026-06-30 but limited team availability (Max, Brody, Gavin briefly present)</p><p>**What&#x27;s Working Well:**</p><p>- Established referral relationships with multiple Dalton Wybar team members who have &quot;form for sending referrals&quot;</p><p>- Paul is proactive in visiting the office and maintaining visibility</p><p>- Email updates being sent to team with product/rate highlights (e.g., First Time Buyer Halifax deals)</p><p>**What&#x27;s Not Working Well:**</p><p>- Referral volume inconsistent – not achieving one referral per week from the team collectively</p><p>- Paul had intended to brief the full team in person on 2026-06-30 but most were unavailable</p><p>**Risks / Blockers:**</p><p>- Inconsistent referral flow creates pipeline volatility</p><p>- Reliance on in-person contact may limit communication effectiveness</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not done |</p><p>| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |</p><p>| Send email update to full Dalton Wybar team with product highlights and commitment to regular updates (assertive tone: &quot;will provide&quot;, not &quot;if you&#x27;re happy&quot;) | Dalton Wybar referral pipeline | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-01 | Referral relationships weaken, visibility declines, undermines authority and commitment |</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Stagnant to Improving** – Paul is maintaining relationships and taking proactive steps, but referral volume remains below target. Email communication strategy may help stabilise flow.</p><p>---</p><p>## 6. COMPLIANCE &amp; FILE QUALITY</p><p>### Case Review Feedback – Product Transfer with Mortgage into Retirement</p><p>**Issue:**</p><p>- Compliance review (Karen at Ray Financial Services) has requested Paul redo a report for a product transfer case</p><p>- Client&#x27;s original mortgage term extends beyond retirement age</p><p>- Paul completed product transfer (path of least resistance client requested) but **cannot vary term** as it&#x27;s a product transfer, not a remortgage</p><p>- Client would not provide affordability documents for full remortgage to vary term</p><p>- Paul had mortgage into retirement conversation but compliance reviewer states this is insufficiently documented</p><p>**What&#x27;s Working Well:**</p><p>- Paul had the conversation with the client about mortgage into retirement</p><p>- Appropriate product (product transfer) was selected based on client circumstances and documentation availability</p><p>**What&#x27;s Not Working Well:**</p><p>- File notes **do not sufficiently evidence** the mortgage into retirement discussion and rationale for product transfer vs remortgage</p><p>- Missing documentation of why remortgage was not suitable and client&#x27;s refusal to provide documents</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not done |</p><p>| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |</p><p>| Review case file notes and add detailed rationale for product transfer covering: mortgage into retirement discussion, client&#x27;s refusal to provide documents, reason remortgage was not viable | Product transfer case (client name not specified) | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-02 | Compliance breach, file marked as inadequate, potential regulatory referral |</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Requires Attention** – Paul is having the right conversations but **file note quality must improve** to evidence advice process. This is a recurring compliance risk.</p><p>---</p><p>## 7. OVERALL PERFORMANCE DIAGNOSIS</p><p>### Adviser Strengths This Week</p><p>- Attempting to work tasks properly rather than just diarising forward for appearance</p><p>- Willing to bin genuinely dead leads rather than letting them sit</p><p>- Making phone contact attempts before email where possible</p><p>- Engaged in learning new CRM workflows</p><p>- Proactive CRM task management and hygiene during review sessions</p><p>- Strong compliance awareness (correctly identified Gary Fraser BTL conversion risk)</p><p>- Maintaining business development relationships (Dalton Wybar)</p><p>- Seeking guidance on complex cases before proceeding</p><p>- Methodical and compliance-conscious when uncertain</p><p>- Strong ethical boundaries (willing to decline business that creates regulatory risk)</p><p>### Main Risks</p><p>- **27 platform tasks with 8 late** represents significant pipeline management failure and SLA breaches</p><p>- **CRM discipline is poor** – tasks incorrectly categorised; workflow stages don&#x27;t accurately reflect case status</p><p>- **Excuse-led behaviour** – justifying backlogs with Edinburgh trip and &quot;these aren&#x27;t massive priority&quot; rather than proactive management</p><p>- **Slow conversion velocity** – cases like Anna (1+ year old) show insufficient urgency and progression</p><p>- **Time wasted on low-probability cases** – Cole case should have been parked much earlier</p><p>- **File note quality and evidencing advice rationale** – compliance review feedback suggests insufficient documentation of key conversations</p><p>- **Inconsistent referral pipeline** – reliant on multiple sources but volume remains below optimal</p><p>- **CRM workflow inefficiencies** creating admin burden</p><p>### Behaviour Patterns</p><p>- **Justification over accountability** – explaining why tasks are late rather than preventing lateness</p><p>- **Workflow confusion** – not fully utilising or understanding CRM tools available</p><p>- **Inconsistent follow-up quality** – some cases receiving attention, others drifting significantly</p><p>- Promise made to clear tasks not delivered</p><p>- Lateness normalised rather than treated as critical issue</p><p>- Risk that without immediate corrective action, task volume will continue growing</p><p>- Needs to strengthen written evidence of advice process in CRM</p><p>### Accountability Concerns</p><p>- Multiple late tasks creating SLA breaches</p><p>- Cases over 1 year old with minimal progress</p><p>- File note quality not meeting compliance standards</p><p>- Excuses for delays rather than proactive prevention</p><p>### Where Management Support Is Required</p><p>- **CRM system review** – duplicate task generation on case progression needs technical resolution</p><p>- **File note training/templates** – to improve compliance review outcomes</p><p>- **Bob to confirm:** New workflow training appears incomplete – Paul needs clearer guidance on when to use &quot;awaiting documents,&quot; nurture stages, and handoff to admin team</p><p>- **Monitor closely:** Task list must reduce to under 15 by 2026-07-04 or further intervention required</p><p>---</p><p>## 8. MASTER ACTION LIST</p><p>| **Action** | **Client / Area** | **Owner** | **Deadline** | **Risk if not done** |</p><p>| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |</p><p>| Clear all 8 late platform tasks | Platform task management | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-02 | Further SLA breaches; clients go cold; conversion loss |</p><p>| Reduce total platform tasks to under 15 | Platform task management | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-04 | Pipeline continues to drift; revenue risk increases |</p><p>| Contact Anna to arrange DIP call; if no response move to Scotland nurture | Anna (Shetland/Glasgow purchase) | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-03 | 1+ year lead becomes dead; opportunity lost |</p><p>| Clarify Jimmy F document status and progress case | Jimmy F | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-02 | Case stalls; unclear ownership creates gaps |</p><p>| Review all 27 tasks and ensure workflow stages accurately reflect case status | CRM discipline | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-03 | Incorrect data; poor handoffs; management can&#x27;t identify real risks |</p><p>| Send final email to Cole; if no response by 2026-07-04, mark lost and archive | Cole (age 69 purchase) | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-02 | Time wasted on dead case |</p><p>| No platform tasks to be late beyond this date | Task discipline | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-04 | Culture of lateness becomes normalised; performance declines |</p><p>| Research refurbishment mortgage options and contact Gary Fraser with compliant alternatives or decline to proceed | Gary Fraser | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-07 | Regulatory breach, reputational damage, loss of authorisation |</p><p>| Add detailed file notes to product transfer case evidencing mortgage into retirement discussion and rationale | Product transfer case (compliance review) | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-02 | Compliance breach, file inadequacy, potential regulatory referral |</p><p>| Send email update to Dalton Wybar team with product highlights and commitment to regular updates (assertive tone, not conditional) | Dalton Wybar referral pipeline | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-01 | Referral flow declines, visibility weakens |</p><p>| Ensure all new tasks include clear next action in brackets and are self-assigned | CRM discipline (all cases) | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Task drift, missed follow-ups |</p><p>| Contact client re: outstanding matter | Anna&#x27;s client | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-01 14:00 | Client disengagement |</p><p>---</p><p>## EMAIL RECAP</p><p>**Subject:** Weekly Performance Review and Agreed Actions – 2026-07-01</p><p>Hi Paul,</p><p>Below is a summary of this week&#x27;s review and agreed actions.</p><p>### Performance Snapshot</p><p>**Platform Tasks:**</p><p>- Current position: 27 tasks (8 late, 5 of which are from 2026-07-01)</p><p>- You&#x27;ve made progress in working through tasks properly rather than just diarising them forward, which is positive</p><p>- However, the volume remains too high and lateness is creating SLA breaches and conversion risk</p><p>**What is working:**</p><p>- Proper task management approach (calling before emailing, binning genuinely dead leads)</p><p>- Engagement with new CRM workflows</p><p>- Strong compliance awareness (correctly identifying regulatory risks)</p><p>- Maintaining business development relationships</p><p>**Identified risks:**</p><p>- High task volume (27) and lateness (8) represents poor pipeline control</p><p>- CRM workflow stages not accurately reflecting case status (e.g., Anna marked as &quot;documents requested&quot; when none requested)</p><p>- Cases drifting (Anna 1+ year old, Cole disengaged)</p><p>- Excuse-led behaviour rather than proactive prevention</p><p>- File note quality not meeting compliance standards</p><p>### Cases Requiring Attention</p><p>**Anna (Shetland/Glasgow):**</p><p>- Lead over 1 year old; previous DIP declined on credit score</p><p>- Last contact 2026-06-18; trying to arrange DIP before summer</p><p>- **Action:** Contact by 2026-07-03 to arrange DIP; if no response, move to Scotland nurture</p><p>**Jimmy F:**</p><p>- Documents status unclear</p><p>- **Action:** Clarify and progress by 2026-07-02</p><p>**Cole (age 69):**</p><p>- Client indicated he&#x27;s speaking to another broker</p><p>- **Action:** One final email by 2026-07-02; if no response by 2026-07-04, mark lost</p><p>**Gary Fraser (First Time Buyer):**</p><p>- Client wants to purchase residential then convert to BTL within 12 months – not compliant</p><p>- **Action:** Research refurbishment mortgage options; contact client with compliant alternatives or decline to proceed by 2026-07-07</p><p>**Product Transfer Case (Compliance Review):**</p><p>- File notes insufficient to evidence mortgage into retirement discussion</p><p>- **Action:** Add detailed rationale by 2026-07-02</p><p>### Dalton Wybar Referral Pipeline</p><p>- Referral volume inconsistent – not achieving target of one referral per person per month</p><p>- **Action:** Send email update to full team with product highlights and commitment to regular updates by 2026-07-01</p><p>### Focus for Next Week</p><p>1. **Eliminate all late tasks** by end of 2026-07-02</p><p>2. **Reduce total platform tasks to under 15** by 2026-07-04</p><p>3. **Ensure CRM workflow stages accurately reflect case status** – review all 27 tasks by 2026-07-03</p><p>4. **No tasks to be late beyond 2026-07-04** – this must become the standard</p><p>5. **Stop justifying delays** – focus on prevention and proactive management</p><p>6. **Strengthen file note quality** – ensure all client conversations, particularly complex advice rationale and product suitability discussions, are fully documented in CRM</p><p>7. **Establish regular email communication rhythm** with Dalton Wybar team to stabilise referral pipeline</p><p>### Agreed Actions</p><p>| **Action** | **Owner** | **Deadline** |</p><p>| --- | --- | --- |</p><p>| Clear all 8 late platform tasks | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-02 |</p><p>| Reduce platform tasks to under 15 | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-04 |</p><p>| Contact Anna re DIP; move to nurture if no response | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-03 |</p><p>| Clarify Jimmy F status and progress | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-02 |</p><p>| Review all tasks and correct workflow stages | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-03 |</p><p>| Final email to Cole; archive if no response | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-02 |</p><p>| Zero late tasks from this point forward | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-04 |</p><p>| Research refurbishment options and contact Gary Fraser | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-07 |</p><p>| Add detailed file notes to product transfer case | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-02 |</p><p>| Send email update to Dalton Wybar team | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-01 |</p><p>| Contact Anna&#x27;s client re outstanding matter | Paul Banyard | 2026-07-01 14:00 |</p><p>| If circumstances change during the week, CRM must be updated immediately and blockers flagged early. |  |  |</p><p>| Bob |  |  |</p><p>---</p><p>**Next Review:** 2026-07-08</p><p>### ADVISER WEEKLY PERFORMANCE &amp; ACTION REVIEW</p><p># INTERNAL PERFORMANCE REVIEW</p><p>## Paul Banyard – Performance Review – 1st July 2026</p><p>---</p><p># 1. FOLLOW-UP FROM PREVIOUS REVIEW</p><p>## Actions Previously Agreed</p><p>Paul committed to clearing his platform task queue, which stood at 27 tasks.</p><p>## Completed Actions</p><p>Paul has made demonstrable progress:</p><p>- Reduced tasks from 27 to 8 (70% reduction)</p><p>- Cleared approximately 19 tasks from the original 27</p><p>- Removed 2-3 cases where multiple contact attempts failed and clients were unresponsive</p><p>- Actively worked cases rather than simply moving them forward without genuine progression</p><p>## Outstanding Actions</p><p>The task queue has not been fully cleared. As of 1st July 2026, Paul has:</p><p>- 8 tasks remaining in his queue</p><p>- 5 tasks are now overdue (due 30th June 2026)</p><p>- Remaining tasks are recent or from 30th June 2026</p><p>## Reasons for Delays</p><p>Paul stated he was in Edinburgh, which impacted his ability to complete all tasks. He also acknowledged he could have made the queue &quot;look prettier&quot; by simply diarising follow-ups rather than actively working cases, but chose to work them properly instead of creating the appearance of completion.</p><p>## Behavioural Patterns</p><p>**Positive:** Paul is attempting to work cases properly rather than manipulating the system to show false progress. He is making phone calls, sending emails where clients are unavailable, and removing dead leads appropriately.</p><p>**Concern:** Paul is still carrying overdue tasks and has not fully delivered on his commitment to clear the queue, though his approach is more constructive than previous patterns suggest.</p><p>---</p><p># 2. LEADS PERFORMANCE REVIEW (INCLUDING CRM DISCIPLINE)</p><p>## A. Platform Tasks – Current Position</p><p>**Current Position:**</p><p>- 8 tasks remaining as of 1st July 2026</p><p>- 5 tasks overdue (due 30th June 2026)</p><p>- Remaining tasks are current or from 30th June 2026</p><p>**Comparison with Last Review:**</p><p>- Previous review: 27 tasks</p><p>- Current review: 8 tasks</p><p>- Reduction: 19 tasks (70% decrease)</p><p>**What&#x27;s Working Well:**</p><p>- Paul is working tasks thoroughly rather than simply moving them through workflows</p><p>- Dead leads are being removed appropriately after multiple contact attempts</p><p>- Paul is making phone calls as first contact method, then following up with email</p><p>**Areas Requiring Improvement:**</p><p>- 5 tasks became overdue on 30th June 2026</p><p>- Tasks are not being cleared within expected timeframes despite commitment</p><p>- Workflow management still requires improvement to prevent overdue accumulation</p><p>- CRM task duplication occurred on Gary Fraser case, requiring manual correction</p><p>**Risks and Blockers:**</p><p>- Client availability (some clients not responding to contact attempts)</p><p>- Paul&#x27;s diary commitments (Edinburgh trip impacted task completion)</p><p>- Time investment required for proper case working versus volume of tasks</p><p>**Actions Required:**</p><p>- Clear all 8 remaining tasks by close of business 5th July 2026</p><p>- Prioritise overdue tasks first</p><p>- Update CRM immediately when client contact fails or cases stall</p><p>**Action Owner:**</p><p>Paul Banyard (Adviser)</p><p>**Trend Analysis:**</p><p>**Improving** – Paul has reduced his task backlog by 70% and is demonstrating better quality of work on individual cases, though deadline adherence remains inconsistent.</p><p>---</p><p># 3. APPLICATIONS IN PROGRESS</p><p>## HSBC Application – US Resident Client</p><p>**Client Name:** Not specified in discussion</p><p>**Lender:** HSBC</p><p>**Current Stage:** Declined at keying stage / underwriter review</p><p>**Outstanding Documents or Delays:**</p><p>- Application declined because clients already own two properties</p><p>- HSBC policy restricts ownership to maximum two properties unless high net worth exception applies</p><p>- Underwriter incorrectly claimed application was resubmitted (joint to sole), which did not occur</p><p>- Underwriter generated false narrative of potential mortgage fraud (claiming buy-to-let being disguised as residential)</p><p>**SLA Breaches:** None directly attributable to Paul or the business</p><p>**Adviser Responsibilities:**</p><p>- Submitted high net worth exception form demonstrating client capability</p><p>- Challenged underwriter decision via escalation to Stacey (HSBC senior contact)</p><p>**Current Blockers:**</p><p>- HSBC underwriter inexperience and incorrect application of policy</p><p>- HSBC keying process auto-declines at two-property threshold</p><p>- Lender error created false fraud concern</p><p>**Next Action Required:**</p><p>- Await response from Stacey following escalation</p><p>- Expect HSBC to reconsider application under high net worth exception</p><p>**Evidence of Good Practice:**</p><p>Bob Duncan provided direct support and escalated appropriately. Paul Banyard received guidance on HSBC policy and high net worth exceptions.</p><p>---</p><p>## Anna Campbell – DIP Required, Documents Received</p><p>**Client Name:** Anna Campbell</p><p>**Lender:** To be determined</p><p>**Current Stage:** Documents received late on 30th June 2026; DIP not yet processed</p><p>**Outstanding Documents or Delays:**</p><p>- Documents received, but DIP has not yet been submitted</p><p>- Task overdue (due 30th June 2026)</p><p>**Adviser Responsibilities:**</p><p>- Process DIP today (1st July 2026)</p><p>- Client could become active property looker following DIP approval</p><p>**Next Action Required:**</p><p>- Submit DIP application immediately</p><p>**Action Owner:** Paul Banyard (Adviser)</p><p>---</p><p>## Gary Fraser – Documents Received, DIP Required</p><p>**Client Name:** Gary Fraser</p><p>**Lender:** To be determined</p><p>**Current Stage:** Client returned to the US; emailed Paul on 30th June 2026; documents received but fact-find incomplete</p><p>**Outstanding Documents or Delays:**</p><p>- Fact-find not yet completed</p><p>- DIP cannot proceed until fact-find done</p><p>- CRM had generated duplicate tasks (ID check, pre-checklist) despite case not having progressed past DIP stage</p><p>**Adviser Responsibilities:**</p><p>- Respond to Gary&#x27;s email</p><p>- Arrange call or ensure fact-find is completed</p><p>- Process DIP once fact-find complete</p><p>**Current Blockers:**</p><p>- Fact-find outstanding preventing DIP submission</p><p>**Next Action Required:**</p><p>- Contact Gary Fraser today (1st July 2026) to arrange fact-find completion</p><p>- Progress DIP and manage task workflow appropriately</p><p>**Action Owner:** Paul Banyard (Adviser)</p><p>---</p><p>## Jimena F – Documents Requested</p><p>**Client Name:** Jimena F</p><p>**Lender:** To be determined</p><p>**Current Stage:** Initial contact made 18th June 2026; client plans to start property search after summer; no specific documents requested yet; DIP required to check credit scoring</p><p>**Outstanding Documents or Delays:**</p><p>- Client not planning active search until after summer (likely August 2026)</p><p>- DIP required as initial attempt over a year ago was declined due to credit scoring</p><p>**Adviser Responsibilities:**</p><p>- Re-contact client to arrange DIP</p><p>- Explain benefit of obtaining DIP now even if not actively searching until later</p><p>**Current Blockers:**</p><p>- Client availability and engagement</p><p>- Client timeline (not searching until post-summer)</p><p>**Next Action Required:**</p><p>- Follow up with client again to arrange DIP appointment</p><p>- If no response, move to nurture workflow with &quot;Active Property Lookers – Scotland&quot; status and &quot;Nurture Reason: Plans to Buy After Summer&quot;</p><p>**Action Owner:** Paul Banyard (Adviser)</p><p>**Notes:**</p><p>Bob Duncan provided training on new workflow process during this review:</p><p>- If documents are required but client is not ready to proceed, use &quot;Request Documents&quot; workflow action</p><p>- If client is not ready to proceed and is in a nurture phase, update lead status to &quot;Active Property Lookers – Scotland&quot; and set nurture reason appropriately</p><p>- Client will then receive monthly communications from Paul regarding the Scottish market</p><p>---</p><p>## Cole – Age 69, Term to Age 80+ Required</p><p>**Client Name:** Cole</p><p>**Lender:** LiveMore identified as option</p><p>**Current Stage:** Initial appointment held last week; Paul attempted phone contact but could not reach client; email sent with lender option</p><p>**Outstanding Documents or Delays:**</p><p>- Client is 69 years old, seeking mortgage term to age 80</p><p>- Client has well-paid employed income (non-manual, office-based role)</p><p>- Most high street lenders will not use employed income beyond age 75</p><p>- LiveMore can provide term to age 80 for non-manual workers, but rates are approximately 6–6.5%</p><p>- Client claims another broker has a lender solution and has disengaged</p><p>**Adviser Responsibilities:**</p><p>- Paul provided accurate advice regarding lender limitations and identified LiveMore as viable option</p><p>**Current Blockers:**</p><p>- Client has engaged another broker</p><p>- Client expectation may not align with market reality</p><p>**Next Action Required:**</p><p>- Do not chase further</p><p>- Remove from active pipeline</p><p>**Action Owner:** Paul Banyard (Adviser) / Bob Duncan (Manager)</p><p>**Risk Assessment:**</p><p>Bob Duncan advised not to waste further time chasing this client. The case should be closed or moved to lost status.</p><p>---</p><p>## Gary (First Time Buyer - Son of Previous Client)</p><p>**Client Name:** Gary (First Time Buyer)</p><p>**Lender:** Initially considering standard residential options; refurbishment mortgage products now being explored</p><p>**Current Stage:** Initial advice stage</p><p>**Customer Intent:** Purchase residential property as First Time Buyer, refurbish, then remortgage as Buy To Let within 12 months</p><p>**Compliance Risk Identified:** Customer&#x27;s stated intention to convert to Buy To Let immediately after purchase on a tracker rate represents a material misrepresentation of intent to lenders</p><p>**Outstanding Actions:** </p><p>- Research refurbishment mortgage products (anticipated LTV: 70-75%)</p><p>- Client conversation required to manage expectations regarding regulatory and lender requirements</p><p>- Adviser must not proceed with residential mortgage where stated intent is immediate Buy To Let conversion</p><p>**Adviser Responsibilities:** </p><p>- Investigate appropriate refurbishment lending options</p><p>- Advise client that proceeding with residential mortgage with stated Buy To Let intent risks adviser&#x27;s regulatory licence</p><p>- Clearly document advice and customer&#x27;s actual intentions</p><p>**Risks and Blockers:** </p><p>- Customer may have insufficient funds to meet LTV requirements for refurbishment products (currently 85-90% LTV on residential; refurbishment likely 70-75% LTV)</p><p>- Customer needs to add significant value through refurbishment to reduce LTV to acceptable Buy To Let levels (75-80%)</p><p>- Regulatory risk if case proceeds as originally discussed</p><p>**Next Action Required:** Paul to contact customer with compliant advice options or decline to proceed</p><p>**Action Owner:** Paul Banyard (Adviser)</p><p>**What&#x27;s Working Well:**</p><p>Paul identified the compliance risk and escalated appropriately before proceeding, demonstrating strong regulatory awareness.</p><p>---</p><p>## Anna (Customer Requiring Contact)</p><p>**Client Name:** Anna</p><p>**Lender:** To be determined</p><p>**Current Stage:** Awaiting adviser contact</p><p>**Outstanding Actions:** Customer contact required; email received late on 30th June 2026</p><p>**Task Created:** Follow-up task set for 1st July 2026 at 14:00</p><p>**CRM Management:** Task assigned to Paul; lead moved to appropriate pipeline stage</p><p>**Next Action Required:** Paul to contact customer on 1st July 2026</p><p>**Action Owner:** Paul Banyard (Adviser)</p><p>---</p><p>### Case Progression Summary</p><p>**What&#x27;s Working Well:**</p><p>- Paul identified the compliance risk with the Gary (First Time Buyer) case and escalated appropriately before proceeding</p><p>- CRM tasks being created and managed to ensure follow-up actions are tracked</p><p>- Paul is proactively reviewing pipeline and identifying blockers</p><p>**Areas Requiring Improvement:**</p><p>- DIP processing speed: cases with documents received (Anna Campbell, Gary Fraser) have not yet been actioned</p><p>- Case progression on Gary Fraser has stalled at DIP stage</p><p>- Referral received from Gavin in March 2026 but not submitted until June 2026 (three-month lag indicates potential pipeline velocity issue)</p><p>**Risks and Blockers:**</p><p>- **Regulatory Risk:** Gary (First Time Buyer) case cannot proceed as originally discussed by customer without significant compliance breach</p><p>- **Pipeline Risk:** Gary Fraser case stalled pending DIP completion</p><p>- **Revenue Risk:** Delayed DIP submissions for Anna Campbell and Gary Fraser who are both ready to become active property lookers</p><p>- **CRM Data Quality:** Task duplication suggests possible workflow or user error requiring attention</p><p>**Trend Analysis:**</p><p>**Stagnant** – Limited case progression; multiple cases have blockers preventing forward movement (DIP pending on Gary Fraser and Anna Campbell; compliance concern on Gary First Time Buyer requiring product/approach change; three-month lag from referral to submission)</p><p>---</p><p># 5. PROTECTION IN PROGRESS</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>One protection review case was discussed requiring follow-up action.</p><p>### Case Detail</p><p>**Karen Ray Financial Services (Remortgage/Product Transfer)**</p><p>**Current Stage:** Protection review required following compliance feedback</p><p>**Issue Identified:** Compliance review has requested additional information regarding mortgage term extending beyond retirement age</p><p>**Original Case Details:** </p><p>- Product transfer completed (not remortgage to new lender)</p><p>- Term extends beyond retirement</p><p>- Customer did not provide affordability documents</p><p>- Customer requested &quot;path of least resistance&quot; via product transfer rather than full remortgage</p><p>**Compliance Feedback:** Reviewer requires Paul to revisit customer and redo suitability report to address mortgage into retirement</p><p>**Adviser Position:** </p><p>- Term could not be varied as this was a product transfer</p><p>- Some lenders (e.g., Halifax) allow term variations on product transfers but require affordability documents, which customer refused to provide</p><p>- Adviser believed mortgage into retirement rationale was documented in original advice</p><p>**Outstanding Actions:** Paul to review case notes and supplement suitability report to satisfy compliance requirements</p><p>**Next Action Required:** Review file, update suitability report with mortgage into retirement justification, resubmit to compliance</p><p>**Action Owner:** Paul Banyard (Adviser)</p><p>### Areas Requiring Improvement</p><p>- Suitability report documentation did not satisfy compliance review on first submission</p><p>- Mortgage into retirement rationale requires clearer articulation in advice documentation</p><p>### Risks and Blockers</p><p>- Compliance review holding case until suitability report updated</p><p>- Potential repeat pattern if advice documentation standards not strengthened</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>- Paul to review case file and suitability report</p><p>- Update report to include clear mortgage into retirement rationale, customer&#x27;s refusal to provide documents for remortgage, and justification for product transfer route</p><p>- Resubmit to compliance</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>Paul Banyard (Adviser)</p><p>### Trend Analysis</p><p>**Requires Monitoring** – Single case discussed; insufficient data to identify broader trend, but compliance feedback indicates advice documentation standards require attention to avoid repeat reviews</p><p>---</p><p># 6. WRITTEN BUSINESS – THIS MONTH</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>One mortgage application submitted on 30th June 2026.</p><p>### Case Detail</p><p>**Source:** Referral from Gavin (Dalton Y team member), originally received March 2026</p><p>**Submission Date:** 30th June 2026</p><p>**Follow-up Action Taken:** Paul spoke with Gavin in person on 30th June 2026 and sent follow-up email to Gavin, copying John Wybar</p><p>**Referral Target Discussed:** One referral per person per Dalton Y team member</p><p>### What&#x27;s Working Well</p><p>- Referral relationship with Dalton Y generating business (Gavin, Brody, Max have all previously referred)</p><p>- Paul is proactively following up with referrers and acknowledging referrals</p><p>### Areas Requiring Improvement</p><p>- Referral received in March 2026 but not submitted until June 2026 (three-month lag)</p><p>- Referral volume from Dalton Y inconsistent – Paul noted they &quot;can&#x27;t even get me one a week&quot;</p><p>### Risks and Blockers</p><p>- Insufficient referral volume to sustain consistent pipeline</p><p>- Long lead time from referral receipt to mortgage submission</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>- Paul to send update email to full Dalton Y team regarding current mortgage products, recent case examples, and referral expectations</p><p>- Email to be sent imminently (Paul indicated he would send &quot;just now&quot; during meeting on 1st July 2026)</p><p>- Email to state ongoing regular updates will be provided (not &quot;if you&#x27;re happy&quot; but definitive commitment)</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>Paul Banyard (Adviser)</p><p>### Trend Analysis</p><p>**Improving** – Paul is taking proactive steps to strengthen referral relationships through in-person contact, follow-up emails, and commitment to regular updates; however, referral volume remains below target and requires sustained focus</p><p>---</p><p># 9. REMORTGAGE OPPORTUNITIES</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>Paul attended Dalton Y office on 30th June 2026 to engage with potential referral sources.</p><p>### Activity Summary</p><p>**Date:** 30th June 2026</p><p>**Location:** Dalton Y office</p><p>**Team Members Present:** Max (briefly), Brody (in and out), Gavin (briefly)</p><p>**Engagement:** Paul spoke with Gavin regarding recent referral conversion and referral expectations</p><p>**Planned Communication:** Email to full Dalton Y team to be sent 1st July 2026 covering:</p><p>- Current mortgage products (e.g., Halifax First Time Buyer deal referenced)</p><p>- Referral expectations (one per person per Dalton Y team member)</p><p>- Regular ongoing updates</p><p>**Previous Communication:** Paul had sent product updates to Dalton Y team before annual leave (June 2026)</p><p>### What&#x27;s Working Well</p><p>- Paul is making in-person visits to Dalton Y office when team members are present</p><p>- Paul is maintaining communication via email updates on products and opportunities</p><p>- Paul is setting clear referral expectations with team members</p><p>### Areas Requiring Improvement</p><p>- Office attendance by Dalton Y team members was limited on 30th June 2026, preventing face-to-face briefing with full team</p><p>- Referral volume remains inconsistent despite ongoing engagement efforts</p><p>### Re-engagement Strategy</p><p>- Transition from in-person briefing to email update due to team availability</p><p>- Commitment to regular (not one-off) email updates to maintain visibility</p><p>- Clear statement of referral expectations: one referral per person per Dalton Y team member</p><p>### Risks and Blockers</p><p>- Dalton Y team availability limiting face-to-face engagement opportunities</p><p>- Referral volume insufficient to meet pipeline requirements despite Paul&#x27;s proactive efforts</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>- Send email to full Dalton Y team on 1st July 2026 (as discussed)</p><p>- Continue regular product and opportunity updates to maintain referrer engagement</p><p>- Monitor referral conversion rates and volume over coming weeks</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>Paul Banyard (Adviser)</p><p>### Trend Analysis</p><p>**Improving** – Paul demonstrating consistent proactive engagement with referral source through multiple channels (in-person, email, post-conversion follow-up); strategy is sound but results require monitoring to assess effectiveness</p><p>---</p><p># 11. OVERALL PERFORMANCE DIAGNOSIS</p><p>## Adviser Strengths</p><p>- **Compliance Awareness:** Paul correctly identified regulatory risk with the Gary (First Time Buyer) case where customer&#x27;s stated intention to immediately convert residential mortgage to Buy To Let would constitute misrepresentation to lenders. Paul escalated appropriately and is seeking compliant alternative solutions rather than proceeding with unsuitable advice.</p><p>- **Task Quality Improvement:** Paul is now working tasks thoroughly rather than manipulating the system to show false progress. He is making genuine contact attempts (phone first, then email) and removing unresponsive leads appropriately.</p><p>- **Referral Relationship Management:** Paul is proactively managing referral relationships with Dalton Y team through in-person visits, post-conversion follow-up, and planned regular email updates on products and opportunities.</p><p>- **Task Clearance Progress:** Paul has reduced his task backlog by 70% (from 27 to 8 tasks) and is demonstrating better quality of work on individual cases.</p><p>## Areas Requiring Improvement</p><p>- **Deadline Adherence:** 5 tasks became overdue on 30th June 2026 despite prior commitment to clear the queue. Paul has not fully delivered on his commitment to clear the task queue, though his approach is more constructive than previous patterns.</p><p>- **Case Progression Speed:** Gary Fraser case stalled at DIP stage; Anna Campbell DIP not submitted despite documents received; referral received from Gavin in March 2026 but not submitted until June 2026 (three-month lag indicates pipeline velocity issue).</p><p>- **Advice Documentation Standards:** Compliance review on Karen Ray Financial Services case required suitability report to be revisited and strengthened regarding mortgage into retirement rationale, suggesting initial documentation did not meet expected standards.</p><p>- **CRM Data Quality:** Task duplication occurred on Gary Fraser case, requiring manual intervention to correct workflow. CRM usage requires greater attention to detail.</p><p>## Key Risks</p><p>- **Regulatory Risk:** Gary (First Time Buyer) case presents material compliance risk if progressed as originally discussed by customer; Paul must either source compliant product (refurbishment mortgage) or decline to proceed.</p><p>- **Revenue Risk:** </p><p>  - Delayed DIP submissions (Anna Campbell and Gary Fraser are both ready to become active property lookers)</p><p>  - Insufficient referral volume from Dalton Y (Paul noted &quot;can&#x27;t even get me one a week&quot;) may impact future pipeline and revenue</p><p>  - 5 overdue tasks represent potential lost conversion opportunities if not actioned promptly</p><p>- **Pipeline Velocity:** Three-month lag between referral receipt and mortgage submission (Gavin case) suggests cases may be progressing slowly through pipeline.</p><p>- **Compliance Standards:** Repeat advice documentation reviews create operational cost and delay; stronger initial documentation required to avoid repeat compliance reviews.</p><p>- **Client Experience:** 5 overdue tasks create client experience risk from slow response times.</p><p>## Behavioural Trends</p><p>- **Improving:** Paul is no longer simply diarising tasks to clear the queue cosmetically; he is working them properly and removing dead leads appropriately.</p><p>- **Improving:** Paul is demonstrating consistent proactive behaviour in managing referral sources, including in-person contact, written follow-up, and planned regular communication.</p><p>- **Improving:** Paul is appropriately escalating cases with regulatory concerns rather than proceeding with potentially unsuitable advice (compliance-conscious decision making).</p><p>- **Concern:** Paul continues to carry overdue tasks and execution is not yet matching intent, despite improved attitude.</p><p>- **Positive:** Paul is engaging constructively with process training and applying it in real time.</p><p>- **CRM Discipline Inconsistency:** While tasks are being created, workflow errors (task duplication) suggest CRM usage requires greater attention to detail.</p><p>## Accountability Concerns</p><p>Paul acknowledged he could have made the queue &quot;look prettier&quot; but chose to work cases properly instead. This demonstrates improved intent and attitude. However, the result is still 8 tasks remaining (5 overdue), which indicates execution is not yet matching intent.</p><p>No significant accountability concerns beyond deadline adherence. Paul is taking ownership of actions, escalating appropriately, and demonstrating awareness of regulatory obligations.</p><p>## Where Management Support or Intervention Is Required</p><p>- **Product Knowledge:** Paul requires support identifying suitable refurbishment mortgage products for Gary (First Time Buyer) case to provide compliant alternative to customer&#x27;s stated intent.</p><p>- **Advice Documentation Standards Review:** Management should review suitability report standards with Paul to reduce likelihood of repeat compliance reviews on mortgage into retirement and similar technical advice points.</p><p>- **Ongoing Monitoring:** Continued monitoring of task queue required to ensure tasks do not accumulate again. Paul requires clear, time-bound expectations with follow-up accountability.</p><p>- **Real-time Training:** Bob Duncan provided real-time workflow training during this review, which Paul applied immediately. This approach appears effective and should continue.</p><p>---</p><p># 12. MASTER ACTION LIST</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not completed |</p><p>|--------|---------------|-------|----------|----------------------|</p><p>| Submit DIP application | Anna Campbell | Paul Banyard | 1st July 2026 | Client cannot become active property looker; revenue opportunity delayed; poor customer experience |</p><p>| Contact client to complete fact-find and submit DIP | Gary Fraser | Paul Banyard | 1st July 2026 | Client disengagement; revenue opportunity lost; case remains stalled |</p><p>| Follow up to arrange DIP or move to nurture workflow | Jimena F | Paul Banyard | 2nd July 2026 | Case drifts; client disengages; no future conversion opportunity |</p><p>| Close or mark as lost | Cole (age 69 case) | Paul Banyard | 2nd July 2026 | Wasted time on unviable lead |</p><p>| Research refurbishment mortgage products and advise customer of compliant options or decline to proceed | Gary (First Time Buyer) | Paul Banyard | 5th July 2026 | Regulatory breach if case proceeds as originally discussed; reputational and licence risk to adviser |</p><p>| Contact customer following late email 30th June 2026 | Anna | Paul Banyard | 1st July 2026 14:00 | Customer disengagement; missed opportunity; poor customer experience |</p><p>| Review case file and update suitability report with mortgage into retirement rationale; resubmit to compli