er) case and escalated appropriately before proceeding. This demonstrates strong regulatory awareness.</p><p>- You are making genuine contact attempts and removing dead leads appropriately.</p><p>- You are engaging constructively with workflow training and applying new processes.</p><p>- Your approach to task management has improved significantly – you are now working cases properly rather than manipulating the system.</p><p>- You are proactively managing your referral relationship with Dalton Y through in-person visits, post-conversion follow-up with Gavin, and planned regular email updates.</p><p>**Risks identified:**</p><p>- 5 overdue tasks create client experience and revenue risk.</p><p>- DIP submissions are delayed for clients who have provided documents (Anna Campbell, Gary Fraser).</p><p>- Cases are drifting without forward movement or appropriate nurture workflow assignment.</p><p>- The Gary (First Time Buyer) case presents material regulatory risk if progressed as the customer originally discussed (residential mortgage with stated immediate Buy To Let conversion intent). You must either source a compliant refurbishment mortgage product or decline to proceed.</p><p>- Case progression speed requires attention: Gary Fraser is stalled at DIP stage, and the Gavin referral took three months from receipt (March 2026) to submission (June 2026).</p><p>- Compliance feedback on the Karen Ray Financial Services case indicates your suitability report requires strengthening regarding mortgage into retirement rationale. This suggests initial documentation did not meet expected standards.</p><p>- Referral volume from Dalton Y remains insufficient to sustain consistent pipeline growth.</p><p>---</p><p>## Cases Requiring Attention</p><p>**Anna Campbell**</p><p>- Issue: Documents received late 30th June 2026; DIP not yet submitted</p><p>- Action: Submit DIP today (1st July 2026)</p><p>- Owner: Paul</p><p>**Gary Fraser**</p><p>- Issue: Client emailed 30th June 2026; documents